# Developing Extensions

Every ArgoPlane extension follows the same pattern: a React/TypeScript UI bundle, a Go backend service, and ArgoCD proxy configuration.

## Extension structure

```
extensions/<name>/
  ui/
    src/
      index.tsx          # Entry point, registers extension
      components/        # React components
      hooks/             # Custom React hooks
      types.ts           # TypeScript types
      api.ts             # Fetch calls to backend
    package.json
    webpack.config.js
    dist/
      extension-<name>.js  # Built bundle (IIFE)
  backend/
    cmd/server/
      main.go            # Entry point
    internal/
      ...                # Domain logic
    go.mod
    Dockerfile
```

## Development workflow

### Prerequisites

- Node.js 20+ (UI extensions)
- Go 1.26+ (backend services)
- kind (local Kubernetes)
- kubectl, helm

### Local setup

```bash
# Full dev environment
make dev-infra

# Build all extensions
make build-extensions

# Deploy to local cluster
make setup-argocd

# Deploy demo app for testing
make deploy-example
```

### Multi-cluster setup (optional)

`make dev-infra` gives you one cluster, where ArgoCD manages itself and every
backend reads the cluster it runs on. That is the right default, but it never
exercises the hub-spoke case: Application CRs live beside ArgoCD while the
reports describing a workload live on the cluster running it.

To get that split, add a spoke:

```bash
# Hub plus spoke in one go
make dev-infra-multicluster

# Or add a spoke to a hub you already have
make spoke

# Remove just the spoke, leaving the hub alone
make spoke-delete
```

The spoke is a second kind cluster running Trivy Operator and a single demo
Deployment from `examples/demo-app/spoke/`. It runs without Cilium, so it gets
its own manifest rather than the hub's demo workloads. It is registered with the hub's ArgoCD as an ordinary cluster
Secret, and a second Application (`argoplane-demo-spoke`) deploys to it.

That Application addresses its destination by **name** rather than server, on
purpose: ArgoCD only sets the `Argocd-Target-Cluster-URL` header when an
Application sets `spec.destination.server`, so a name-addressed destination is
exactly the case a backend reading that header would get wrong. A backend has
to resolve the destination from the Application itself.

`make spoke` also enables `MULTI_CLUSTER` on the vulnerabilities backend and
grants it the Secret read it needs, since neither belongs in a single-cluster
setup. `make spoke-delete` reverts both.

The two Applications deploy to **different namespaces** on purpose
(`argoplane-demo` on the hub, `argoplane-demo-spoke` on the spoke). Sharing one
would mean a backend reading the wrong cluster still found reports with the
right names, so the setup would look healthy while proving nothing. Apart from
that they stay visible side by side, so the single-cluster and multi-cluster
paths can be compared directly. `make clean-all` removes the spoke along with
the hub.

Reports take a few minutes to appear after the spoke comes up:

```bash
kubectl --context kind-argoplane-dev-spoke get vulnerabilityreports -n argoplane-demo-spoke
```

### Building a single extension

After editing UI code:

```bash
cd extensions/<name>/ui && npm run build
make load-extensions
```

After editing backend code:

```bash
make build-<name>
make load-<name>
make deploy-<name>
```

## Checklist for new extensions

1. Create `extensions/<name>/ui/` and `extensions/<name>/backend/`
2. Register the extension via `window.extensionsAPI` in `index.tsx`
3. Build the Go backend HTTP server
4. Add deployment manifests in `deploy/extensions/<name>/`
5. Add proxy config in `deploy/argocd/proxy-extensions.json`
6. Add RBAC in `hack/setup-argocd.sh`
7. Add to `EXTENSIONS` list in the Makefile
8. Update `examples/demo-app/` with test resources
9. Update documentation in `services/docs/`

